# Independently authored PG ALTER MATERIALIZED VIEW
# family + CREATE FUNCTION ... BEGIN ATOMIC sentinel
# slice for the GPL MTR-format runner. NOT copied or
# adapted from MySQL's mysql-test/ or PG regress.
#
# Existing coverage (do NOT duplicate):
#   - CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW (bare) and REFRESH
#     MATERIALIZED VIEW (bare) — view_options_basic_extra
#     (CREATE form parses+materializes; REFRESH bare
#     works; DROP MATERIALIZED VIEW rejects 1064; MV
#     invisible to information_schema).
#   - REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW {CONCURRENTLY | WITH DATA
#     | WITH NO DATA} — pg_materialized_view_refresh_bug
#     _sentinel (token-gobbling bug class).
#   - ALTER MATERIALIZED VIEW family — ZERO prior
#     coverage. Grepped tests/main/*.test: no hits.
#   - CREATE FUNCTION ... BEGIN ATOMIC — ZERO prior
#     coverage. Grepped tests/main/*.test for
#     "BEGIN ATOMIC" and "LANGUAGE SQL BEGIN": no hits.
#     Adjacent: pg_create_function_non_trigger_sentinel
#     pins RETURNS TABLE(...) silent-accept + RETURNS
#     SETOF parse-reject; function_def_basic_extra pins
#     scalar RETURNS forms. Neither exercises a
#     BEGIN ATOMIC body.
#
# Pre-existing rejection shape patterns (per
# fingerprint-dedup discipline):
#   - "Expected: one of VIEW or TYPE or TABLE or INDEX
#     or ROLE or POLICY or CONNECTOR or ICEBERG or
#     SCHEMA or USER or OPERATOR, found: <KW>" at col 7
#     — pre-existing at:
#       event_use_partition_sentinel:101 (kw EVENT —
#         MySQL EVENT scheduler)
#       pg_alter_func_proc_lo_io_sentinel (FUNCTION,
#         PROCEDURE)
#       pg_ddl_tablespace_conversion_group_sentinel
#         (GROUP)
#       PR #395 (DEFAULT)
#       pg_event_trigger_large_object_sentinel:28
#         (LARGE) and the EVENT-trigger duplicate (PR
#         #429)
#     DUPLICATE FINGERPRINT — pinned here as the NEXT
#     witness in the shared ALTER-allowed-list chain.
#     This slice contributes kw MATERIALIZED.
#   - "Expected: end of statement, found: BEGIN at
#     Line: 1, Column: 47" — NEW fingerprint at this
#     slice for CREATE FUNCTION ... BEGIN ATOMIC. The
#     column number is body-position-dependent (47 for
#     the exact prefix `CREATE FUNCTION f1() RETURNS
#     INT LANGUAGE SQL `), but the SHAPE — "Expected:
#     end of statement, found: BEGIN" — is the
#     structural fingerprint: sqlparser exits the
#     CREATE FUNCTION grammar after the LANGUAGE clause
#     and does not recognize the SQL-standard
#     BEGIN ATOMIC ... END body form.
#   - "unknown function: f1" — pre-existing at
#     function_def_basic_extra (sqlfn), PRs #349-#369
#     (function-arm gap closure). DUPLICATE — pinned as
#     the runtime witness that the parse-rejected
#     CREATE FUNCTION was a true no-op (the function
#     never made it into the catalog).
#
# This slice pins:
#
#   ALTER MATERIALIZED VIEW (9 variants):
#     RENAME TO, OWNER TO, SET SCHEMA, SET TABLESPACE,
#     ALTER COLUMN ... SET STATISTICS, SET (storage
#     parameter), IF EXISTS RENAME TO, CLUSTER ON, and
#     ALL IN TABLESPACE ... SET TABLESPACE. All NINE
#     forms reject at the `MATERIALIZED` token with the
#     IDENTICAL ALTER-allowed-list fingerprint at col 7
#     — the trailing clauses are unreached because the
#     gate is at the dispatcher. NEW kw witness
#     MATERIALIZED, joining the documented set: EVENT
#     (PR #429), DEFAULT (PR #395), FUNCTION, PROCEDURE,
#     GROUP, LARGE (PR #429).
#
#   CREATE FUNCTION ... LANGUAGE SQL BEGIN ATOMIC ...
#   END (SQL:2003 standard body form, PG 14+):
#     Parse-rejected at the `BEGIN` token. sqlparser
#     does not recognize the SQL-standard BEGIN ATOMIC
#     body for SQL functions. Real-world relevance:
#     modern pg_dump emits BEGIN ATOMIC for SQL-language
#     functions on PG 14+ targets (because the
#     string-literal AS '<body>' form has dependency-
#     tracking caveats). Apps roundtripping schema
#     through modern pg_dump → zeta will hit this
#     parse-reject; the function is not catalogged
#     (confirmed by subsequent `SELECT f1()` returning
#     "unknown function: f1", not a different "exists
#     but no body" shape).
#
# Big-picture findings:
#
#   STRUCTURAL — "shared ALTER allowed-list" sentinel
#   continues to bind. The 11-keyword ALTER whitelist
#   (VIEW / TYPE / TABLE / INDEX / ROLE / POLICY /
#   CONNECTOR / ICEBERG / SCHEMA / USER / OPERATOR)
#   excludes every PG-only ALTER object family. Every
#   ALTER MATERIALIZED VIEW form rejects at the same
#   gate — there is no path to reach the trailing
#   clause grammar (OWNER / RENAME / SET / CLUSTER /
#   IF EXISTS). This is the documented behavior; no
#   bug, just gap. Witness count for this fingerprint:
#   7 keywords (EVENT, DEFAULT, FUNCTION, PROCEDURE,
#   GROUP, LARGE, MATERIALIZED).
#
#   BEGIN ATOMIC is the SQL-standard SQL-function body
#   form, added in PG 14 (2021). pg_dump's
#   `--no-quote-fn-bodies` flag and the implicit
#   modern-PG default emit BEGIN ATOMIC for SQL fns —
#   so any pg_dump output from a recent PG source will
#   include this shape. zeta's sqlparser-derived parser
#   does not support it; the migration path is either
#   (a) re-dump with older syntax (string-literal body)
#   or (b) rewrite the function in a supported form
#   (RETURNS trigger, per CLAUDE.md). Scalar SQL
#   functions are already a silent-accept gap (per
#   pg_create_function_non_trigger_sentinel), so
#   BEGIN ATOMIC failing earlier — at parse — is
#   actually a less-misleading failure mode than the
#   string-literal AS '<body>' form.
#
# Wire divergence check: the ALTER and CREATE FUNCTION
# dispatchers live in convert.rs (parser-shared). Both
# pgwire and mysqlwire route through the same sqlparser
# entrypoint; the rejection shape comes from sqlparser's
# grammar and is wire-independent. No wire-divergence
# pinning needed here.
#
# Adaptation notes:
#   # zeta-adapt: KNOWN GAP — every ALTER MATERIALIZED
#     VIEW form errors 1064 at the `MATERIALIZED`
#     keyword (ALTER restricted-list gate at col 7).
#     The only supported MV mutation path is
#     CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW (bare) at create-time;
#     post-create ALTER is unreachable. Apps porting PG
#     MV management must drop+recreate instead of
#     ALTER.
#   # zeta-adapt: KNOWN GAP — CREATE FUNCTION ...
#     LANGUAGE SQL BEGIN ATOMIC ... END errors 1064 at
#     the `BEGIN` keyword. Modern pg_dump output for
#     SQL-language functions must be rewritten to the
#     string-literal AS '<body>' form (which silently
#     accepts per pg_create_function_non_trigger_
#     sentinel) or, preferably, to a supported
#     RETURNS trigger form.

--echo === ALTER MATERIALIZED VIEW (9 forms) — ALTER restricted-list "found: MATERIALIZED" at col 7 ===

--error 1064
ALTER MATERIALIZED VIEW mv RENAME TO mv2;
--error 1064
ALTER MATERIALIZED VIEW mv OWNER TO postgres;
--error 1064
ALTER MATERIALIZED VIEW mv SET SCHEMA public;
--error 1064
ALTER MATERIALIZED VIEW mv SET TABLESPACE pg_default;
--error 1064
ALTER MATERIALIZED VIEW mv ALTER COLUMN id SET STATISTICS 100;
--error 1064
ALTER MATERIALIZED VIEW mv SET (autovacuum_enabled = false);
--error 1064
ALTER MATERIALIZED VIEW IF EXISTS mv RENAME TO mv2;
--error 1064
ALTER MATERIALIZED VIEW mv CLUSTER ON idx;
--error 1064
ALTER MATERIALIZED VIEW ALL IN TABLESPACE old_ts SET TABLESPACE pg_default;

--echo === CREATE FUNCTION ... BEGIN ATOMIC (SQL-standard body, PG 14+, pg_dump-emitted) — "found: BEGIN" ===

--error 1064
CREATE FUNCTION f1() RETURNS INT LANGUAGE SQL BEGIN ATOMIC SELECT 1; END;

--echo === confirm BEGIN ATOMIC did not register f1 in catalog (parse-reject was a true no-op) ===

--error 1105
SELECT f1();
